uot;&gt;A reader requests a page and the static host returns pre-rendered HTML, with no server in the loop.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;with-javascript-off&quot;&gt;With JavaScript off&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rendering happens in the browser, so if a reader has JavaScript disabled the diagram
does not draw. What survives is the part that carries the meaning: the &lt;strong&gt;raw diagram
source&lt;/strong&gt; stays in the page, and the &lt;strong&gt;caption&lt;/strong&gt; (the &lt;code dir=&quot;auto&quot;&gt;description&lt;/code&gt;) is right below it.
Nothing is lost silently — the reader sees the source and its plain-language summary
instead of a picture. The diagram JavaScript also loads &lt;strong&gt;only&lt;/strong&gt; on pages that
actually contain a diagram, so a diagram-free page pays no cost.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
